European ministers responsible for  energy policy am and Luxembourg to agree  on new rules to make the use of biofuels  in the EU more sustainable and so help  cut greenhouse gas emissions the council  wants to finalize its position on so  called indirect land-use change this is  when farm lands used to produce biofuels  leading to food or feed being grown in  other areas like forests which results  in more carbon dioxide in the atmosphere  the aims to minimize the environmental  impact of indirect land use and  encourage more advanced biofuels onto  the market which will in turn reduce  greenhouse gas emissions energy prices  are also up for discussion the ministers  are set to examine ways to strengthen  the role rights and awareness of  consumers with a special focus on  combating energy poverty and helping  vulnerable consumers preventing the  disproportionate disconnection of  people's electricity is one area of  concern energy security in Europe in  particular avoiding any major disruption  in supplies next winter and the conflict  in Ukraine will also be debated Russia's  the e u--'s biggest natural gas supplier  and much of that gas transits through  Ukraine the two countries are locked in  a dispute over gas prices which is  simmered for several years and has hit  supplies into Europe in the past
 

